Fix conversions between pointer types so that the result behaves like a pointer of the destination type, the way it does in Go. This covers explicit conversions such as `P(p)` or `(*MyInt)(p)`, the implicit conversions that happen wherever a `*T` value is used where an assignable named pointer type such as `type P *T` is expected or the other way around, conversions through `unsafe.Pointer`, and the same operations done through reflection.

A converted pointer has the destination type, as seen by `%T`, reflection and type assertions. It is nil exactly when the source is nil, and otherwise it refers to the same variable as the source, so writes through either pointer are visible through the other. Pointers to the same variable are equal once converted to a common type, whatever types they went through, so converted pointers also work as map keys. This holds whatever the pointer points to, including slice elements and struct fields.

With `unsafe.Pointer`, the zero value converts to the nil pointer of any type and nil pointers convert to it, and pointers to the same variable give equal values. A pointer converted through it to a type whose element has the same memory layout, such as `*int64` to `*MyInt64`, `*[2]int64` to `*[2]MyInt64`, or between struct types whose fields differ that way, follows the rules above, and values read through it have the destination types down to array elements and struct fields, while writes keep the types of the original variable.

In reflection, `Value.Convert`, `NewAt`, and pointers stored by `Set`, `SetMapIndex` or passed to `Call` follow the same rules, and `Value.Pointer` and `UnsafePointer` agree for pointers to the same variable.
